<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SCE Forge conformance: the `round(x)` builtin, and specifically what it does
  at `.5`.

  ⚠ WHY `.5` IS THE WHOLE POINT. The six backends do not agree on rounding
  unless SCE makes them:

      C/C++ `std::llround`, Rust `f64::round`, Go `math.Round`
          half away from zero — 0.5 → 1, 2.5 → 3, −0.5 → −1
      Python `round`, Kotlin `kotlin.math.round`
          half to EVEN — 0.5 → 0, 2.5 → 2

  Every other input agrees. A fixture that feeds 1.4 and 1.6 and calls it
  covered would pass on all six while Python and Kotlin silently disagreed with
  the rest on exactly the inputs a specification's rounding rule is about. So
  the cases below carry 0.5, 2.5 and their negatives, and the expected values
  are the half-away-from-zero ones: SCE lowers Python and Kotlin through
  `floor(x + 0.5)` / `ceil(x - 0.5)` rather than the language builtin, and this
  is what holds that lowering in place.

  ⚠⚠ 2.5 IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH 0.5. Banker's rounding sends 0.5 → 0 (down, to
  even) and 2.5 → 2 (down, to even), while 1.5 → 2 (up, to even) agrees with
  half-away. A fixture carrying only 1.5 would not separate the two rules at
  all.

  The builtin exists because the surveyed source material attaches a rounding
  notation to hundreds of computed values, and once the piecewise-interpolation
  ones are set aside the rest are linear unit conversions of the shape
  `raw * k + c`. The arithmetic already lowered; rounding was the missing piece.
  `floor` is the other half of the same notation — see `transform_floor`.

  `round(x, digits)` is deliberately absent: a second parameter is a different
  signature and a different question about the boundary, and a digit count
  composes anyway as `floor(x * 100) / 100`.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="transform" name="rounding">
  <datamodel>
    <data id="value" sce:type="float64" sce:direction="in"/>
    <data id="rounded" sce:type="int32" sce:direction="out" expr="round(value)"/>
  </datamodel>
</scxml>
